<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yamuna</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Yamuna River, Jumna</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Yamuna is a major river of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and the largest tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>, flowing about 1,370 kilometres from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalaya</a> past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> to join the Ganges at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Prayagraj</a>. The fertile Ganga–Yamuna doab between the two rivers was the heartland of many north Indian states. The river is sacred in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> and closely linked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yamuna.jpg" alt="Yamuna" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Umang108 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vishram_Ghat.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Yamuna rises in the Yamunotri Glacier on the south-western slopes of the Bandarpunch peaks of the Lower Himalaya in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttarakhand">Uttarakhand</a>. It enters <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himachal-pradesh">Himachal Pradesh</a> and then flows into the Indo-Gangetic plains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haryana">Haryana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>, passing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, and drains an area of 366,223 square kilometres, 40.2 per cent of the Ganges Basin. It meets the Ganges at the Triveni Sangam in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Prayagraj</a>, a site of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumbh-mela">Kumbh Mela</a>, which is held every 12 years. Its tributaries include the Tons, its largest, the Chambal, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, the Betwa and the Ken. With the Ganges it forms the fertile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-yamuna-doab">Ganges–Yamuna Doab</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest mention of the Yamuna is in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rig Veda</a>, composed in the Vedic period, and it also appears in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atharvaveda">Atharvaveda</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmanas">Brahmanas</a> such as the Aitareya and Shatapatha. Greek sources know it as Iomanes, in the surveys of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I Nicator</a>, who visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 305 BCE. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> the Pandava capital of Indraprastha stood on its banks, at the site of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>.</p><p>The river's waters have long been used for irrigation. Some canals date to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughlaq-dynasty">Tughlaq dynasty</a> in the 14th century, which built the Nahr-i-Bahisht, or Paradise canal, parallel to the river. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> restored and extended it in the first half of the 17th century under the engineer Ali Mardan Khan, to near the capital Shahjahanabad, the present <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> the river is venerated as the goddess Yamuna, daughter of the sun god Surya and his wife Saranyu and twin sister of Yama, the god of death. According to tradition, bathing in her waters frees one from the torments of death, and the Padma Purana says they cleanse the mind of sin. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rig Veda</a> counts the Yamuna among the seven sacred rivers, and in Gupta art she and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> appear on temple doorjambs to purify those who pass through.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamunotri">Yamunotri</a> temple, dedicated to the goddess near the river's source, is among the holiest shrines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>. The river is closely associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a> tell how he subdued the Naga Kaliya, who had terrorised the people of Braja, and the river is a centre of pilgrimage for his devotees.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yamuna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The land on the banks of the <b>Yamuna</b> suffered frequent dry spells, while the Baiswara did not.</blockquote><a href="/empire/awadh-state">Awadh State · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 786–790 Dhruva crossed the Narmada and defeated both the Pratihara king Vatsaraja and the Pala king Dharmapala in the Ganga–<b>Yamuna</b> doab.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashtrakuta-dynasty">Rashtrakuta dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Old towns were rebuilt and new cities founded on fresh plans, and the same script, stamp seals, weights, bricks, pottery and ornaments appeared from the Makran coast near Iran to the Ganges–<b>Yamuna</b> doab.</blockquote><a href="/culture/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The complex covers 17 hectares on a platform 300 metres long beside the <b>Yamuna</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/taj-mahal">Taj Mahal · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>Firuz Shah dug a canal from the <b>Yamuna</b> to steady the food supply, offered converts freedom from taxes and the jizya, and raised the jizya on those who stayed Hindu.</blockquote><a href="/societies/delhi-sultanate">Social Hierarchy of the Delhi Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bangash nawabs ruled a territory between the Ganges and <b>Yamuna</b> populated by Afghan settlers and Hindu farmers.</blockquote><a href="/state/bangash-nawabs-of-farrukhabad">Bangash Nawabs of Farrukhabad</a></li></ul></section>
